Another stab at the unmount thing. Personally I don't like this patch at all (it's messy), but perhaps someone will have a different opinion, or some ideas about how to improve it.
Thanks, moved the function declaration.
I think it'd be cleaner (or at least more consistent) to follow the pattern for VfsFat.getcwd / os.getcwd.

Also, it should be "umount" not "unmount" as per the HW API (rationale for umount is that it follows the unix name).
> I think it'd be cleaner (or at least more consistent) to follow the pattern for VfsFat.getcwd / os.getcwd.

I don't understand this. I looked at ous.getcwd, and it looks like this:

```
STATIC mp_obj_t os_getcwd(void) {
    return vfs_proxy_call(MP_QSTR_getcwd, 0, NULL);
}
STATIC MP_DEFINE_CONST_FUN_OBJ_0(os_getcwd_obj, os_getcwd);
```

I compared it with my patch:

```
STATIC mp_obj_t os_unmount(void) {
    return vfs_proxy_call(MP_QSTR_unmount, 0, NULL);
}
STATIC MP_DEFINE_CONST_FUN_OBJ_0(os_unmount_obj, os_unmount);
```

Seems to be the same pattern? The `VfsFat` function:

```
STATIC mp_obj_t fat_vfs_getcwd(mp_obj_t vfs_in) {
    (void)vfs_in;
    char buf[MICROPY_ALLOC_PATH_MAX + 1];
    FRESULT res = f_getcwd(buf, sizeof buf);

    if (res != FR_OK) {
        nlr_raise(mp_obj_new_exception_arg1(&mp_type_OSError, MP_OBJ_NEW_SMALL_INT(fresult_to_errno_table[res])));
    }

    return mp_obj_new_str(buf, strlen(buf), false);
}
STATIC MP_DEFINE_CONST_FUN_OBJ_1(fat_vfs_getcwd_obj, fat_vfs_getcwd);
```

is more complex than mine:

```
STATIC mp_obj_t fat_vfs_unmount(mp_obj_t vfs_in) {
    fatfs_umount(((fs_user_mount_t *)vfs_in)->readblocks[1]);
    return mp_const_none;
}
STATIC MP_DEFINE_CONST_FUN_OBJ_1(fat_vfs_unmount_obj, fat_vfs_unmount);
```

Because they return a value, and need to allocate memory for it, and I don't. So again, seems to be the same pattern to me? Sure, `getcwd` calls a function from the fat lib, and I call one from `fsusermount`, but that's can't be it?

Sorry if it's something obvious, I just don't see it.

I will rename the methods.
Sorry, you are right it is the same pattern as getcwd, I didn't read the patch correctly.
Ah, phew, I was really stumped here for a moment. I renamed the methods to `umount`.
